## Original Content
The world largely moved on from the Rohingya crisis. But the exodus has not stopped, aid is shrinking and a generation of refugees is growing up with little prospect of going home.<br />

## Summary
The Rohingya crisis persists nine years after its peak, with global attention fading, yet refugees continue to flee and aid is decreasing. A new generation is growing up in exile with no realistic prospect of returning home, indicating a dangerous and protracted phase of the crisis.

## Key Points
- The world has largely forgotten about the ongoing Rohingya crisis.
- The exodus of Rohingya refugees has not stopped despite the passage of time.
- Humanitarian aid for the refugees is shrinking, exacerbating their plight.
- A generation of refugee children is growing up with little hope of repatriation.


